Instruction: Review the following segment from a merger agreement.

Merger Agreement Segment:
"‘Material Adverse Effect’ (MAE) is defined as any event, change, or occurrence that (i) would either materially delay, impair, or preclude the consummation of the Merger or (ii) would have a material adverse effect on the Company’s overall business, assets, or financial performance. However, with respect to the component described in clause (i), the following carveouts shall apply: 
   • Any adverse effects that arise solely from uniform shifts in general economic conditions or broad fluctuations in financial markets, including changes in prevailing interest and exchange rates, shall be excluded;
   • Natural disasters, force majeure events, and pandemics are excluded unless they result in a disproportionately adverse impact on the Company relative to its industry peers;
   • Actions taken pursuant to written directives or consents from the Parent, effective on or after the Effective Date, are likewise excluded from disturbing MAE assessments.
Notwithstanding the above carveouts, the determination of the Company’s ability to consummate the Merger is expressly made by first applying these carveouts and then assessing the remaining adverse impacts. 

Question: Based on the merger agreement segment provided, is the ‘ability to consummate’ component evaluated independently of the carveouts, or is it subject to the carveouts outlined in the agreement? Options: Option A: It is subject to the carveouts. Option B: It is evaluated independently (i.e., exempt from the carveouts).
