Instruction: Read the following segment of an acquisition agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by selecting the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: In the context of the following agreement, is the 'ability to consummate' the transaction subject to the carveouts provided under the Material Adverse Effect clause?

Acquisition Agreement Excerpt:
"Material Adverse Effect" means any event, change, circumstance or effect which, either individually or in aggregate with all other factors existing prior to the date of determination, has or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse impact on the business, financial condition, assets or operations of the Acquired Party. In determining whether a Material Adverse Effect has occurred, however, no consideration shall be given to:
   (i) effects arising solely from broad-based economic trends or general market volatility affecting the industry as a whole;
   (ii) changes in regulatory or legislative conditions that impact all competitors in a similar fashion;
   (iii) fluctuations in interest rates, exchange rates, or trading volumes of securities, unless such fluctuations are disproportionately severe compared to industry peers;
   (iv) natural disasters, acts of God or other force majeure events. 
Notwithstanding the foregoing, any event that prevents, materially delays or impairs the Acquired Party’s ability to consummate the transaction shall be deemed to fall within the definition of a Material Adverse Effect and shall not benefit from the exceptions enumerated above.

Option A: No
Option B: Yes
