Instruction: Read the segment of the XYZ Acquisition Agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?

Option A: No
Option B: Yes

XYZ Acquisition Agreement: 
For purposes of this Agreement, the term “Company Material Adverse Effect” shall mean any event, change, circumstance or occurrence that, individually or in aggregate, either (i) has a material adverse effect on the financial condition, operations or prospects of the Company or (ii) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay or impair the Company’s ability to consummate the Transaction. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following events shall not be deemed to constitute a Company Material Adverse Effect: 
   (a) any fluctuations in general economic conditions, credit markets, or industry-wide trends; 
   (b) changes in applicable Law or regulatory requirements, including adjustments to accounting standards; 
   (c) variations in market interest rates, exchange rates, or the trading price of the Company’s securities; 
   (d) actions taken by governmental entities that affect the industry as a whole; 
provided, however, that the exclusions set forth in subparagraphs (a) through (d) shall not apply to any event, change, circumstance or occurrence that directly affects the Company’s ability to consummate the Transaction. In such cases, any impact on the ability to consummate the Transaction shall be evaluated without regard to the general carveouts listed above.

Answer the question based solely on the above Agreement segment.
