Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: "Material Adverse Effect" means any event, change, condition or development that, either individually or in the aggregate with other occurrences, has a material adverse impact on the financial condition, business operations, or results of operations of the Company, or that materially impairs the Company’s ability to consummate the transaction. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for purposes of determining whether a Material Adverse Effect has occurred, the following effects shall be excluded:
   (i) Any adverse impact arising solely from fluctuations in foreign exchange rates;
   (ii) Effects attributable to general economic downturns or market volatility affecting the industry broadly;
   (iii) Changes in regulatory requirements that are standard across the industry, even if they impact operational costs;
   (iv) Effects that occur solely as a consequence of the public announcement or pendency of this Agreement; and
   (v) Adjustments resulting from the Controller’s Directive issued on July 15, 2023.
Furthermore, any delay in consummating the transaction due solely to temporary shifts in interest rates, which are consistent with trends observed among peer companies, shall not be considered to materially impair the Company’s consummation ability. However, if such effects are disproportionately adverse when compared to industry benchmarks, they may be considered part of a Material Adverse Effect.
