Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement. Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts? Option A: No; Option B: Yes.

Merger Agreement: “Consummation Condition” shall be deemed satisfied if the Company, its subsidiaries, and affiliates are able to complete the transactions contemplated herein, except to the extent that any adverse effect, event, or condition directly attributable to internal mismanagement, targeted corporate governance failures, or misrepresentations materially impairs the Company’s capability to consummate the transaction. However, for purposes of determining such impairment, the following shall be excluded: (i) impacts arising solely from global economic downturns; (ii) standard fluctuations in interest rates and currency exchange rates; (iii) delays in national regulatory decisions that are not specifically targeted at the Company; and (iv) the occurrence of natural disasters, provided such events do not uniquely burden the Company relative to industry peers. In all circumstances, only those adverse effects outside the scope of these exclusions shall be considered in determining a Material Adverse Effect with respect to the ability to consummate the transactions.
